NEARS-3501 — additional regression point requested by [7] security review (pre-existing,
OUTSIDE this ticket's fix scope; does not affect NEARS-3501's own PASS/FAIL verdict)

Question: as an admin holding only module:custom_role, can they open/edit a DIFFERENT custom role
that isn't their own (not id=1)?

Static finding being confirmed live: CustomRoleUpdateRequest::authorize() returns true
unconditionally (Admin/app/Http/Requests/Admin/CustomRoleUpdateRequest.php); the controller only
calls CustomRoleService::roleCheck($id), which returns unauthorized ONLY for id==1
(Admin/app/Services/CustomRoleService.php:34-41) — no ownership/scope check against the acting
admin's own role id anywhere in the chain.

Live repro (worktree FIXED server, :8010):
Actor: employee id=7 (nears3501-poc-employee@test.local), own role = id=8, holds ONLY
module:custom_role.
Target: admin_roles id=5 "QA Role D Baseline Order Only" — belongs to nobody related to this
employee, not id=1, not their own role.

GET http://127.0.0.1:8010/admin/users/custom-role/edit/5
< HTTP/1.1 200 OK
Response body contains the FULL edit form pre-populated with role id=5's real data:
  name="name[]" id="default_title" ... value="QA Role D Baseline Order Only"
  name="name[]" id="en_title" ... value="QA Role D Baseline Order Only"
(role name appears twice in the rendered form — default + en locale fields; both show role 5's
real name, confirming the server actually loaded and returned that role's full record, not a 404
or an empty/blocked view)

CONFIRMED: true — an admin_employee holding nothing but module:custom_role CAN open/view any
other custom role's full edit form (any id except 1), with no ownership check. This is an IDOR
class finding on Admin\Employee\CustomRoleController's read (and, by the same unconditional
authorize()+roleCheck() logic, presumably the update/delete paths too — not exercised here to
avoid mutating the shared QA fixture role id=5).

Scope note: this is pre-existing behavior, NOT introduced or worsened by NEARS-3501's diff (which
only touches modules[] value validation, not authorization/ownership). Reported as a
regression_bugs finding per the QA task brief, not a NEARS-3501 AC failure.

No PoC data mutation was needed to confirm this (a GET was sufficient); role id=5 was left
untouched.
